selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499388 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499388 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "98f862f6-df7c-5829-b5f0-70de3edf4727" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499391 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499391 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499391 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499391 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a81e5efe-662a-598e-a045-0d6af521e7e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cc2c8709-44f0-5048-b1ab-fe7e6a0dc83d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499443 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499443 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499443 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499443 #edb/ref 1071 "A primary school teacher records how many words his second-year students can read in 5 minutes. The number of words read is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =36$ words. In a school of $180$ students, he finds that the mean number of read words is $255$ words. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the mean number of words $\\mu $ for the entire population of second-year children.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499443 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "094a5111-7dbf-54d8-b6a9-b1a46e16163d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499443 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=180\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=255,\\,n=180,\\,\\sigma =36.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/35/35037db1532092651e1974ea689b6004aa673c29720ac1d1c9d191c5929e9314.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}255\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{180}} \\\\ 255\\pm 5.26\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499458 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499458 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499458 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499458 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=64$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =4.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=8,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499458 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3aeeb390-5562-50bf-80f7-9c922c7da785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499458 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\ 8\\pm 0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499492 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499492 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499492 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499492 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=45$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =43.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-280,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499492 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "795def41-6dcd-54b5-9baf-91c8a014878e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499492 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\si